# The DEFAULT graph-sweep noise list — ccrc's own footprint, and nothing else.
#
# D-1160. `_gs_guard` refuses a build when any path `detect()` picks up is not
# git-tracked, because an untracked file entering the corpus would silently
# change a graph nobody can reproduce. That guard is right. What was wrong is
# that ccrc's OWN tooling writes untracked files into every repo a session
# touches — `.remember/` transcripts, `.superpowers/` specs, `.claude/`
# settings, a `CLAUDE.local.md` — and those files were then held against the
# repo, refusing its build for ever.
#
# Measured on the reference fleet before this list existed: 14 of 57 trees
# refused, 304 breach paths, of which 186 were these four. Five repos were
# blocked by NOTHING BUT ccrc's own artifacts.
#
# THE LINE THIS FILE DRAWS: only what ccrc and its skills create. A repo's own
# untracked files — build output, scratch scripts, uncommitted work — stay a
# breach, because there the guard is doing exactly its job and the answer is to
# track the file or write a per-repo list beside this one.
#
# ccrc OWNS this file and rewrites it on install; a '!' line anywhere in it
# refuses every build on the box, so it carries none. Operator additions belong
# in `<repo>.list` in this same directory, which is unioned with this one.
.claude/
.remember/
.superpowers/
CLAUDE.local.md
